pulse: show a day/night reading on cameras where the streamer has none

The status page asks the streamer for the isp_again metric and hides the
reading when it answers -1. On Ingenic it always answers -1: majestic never
fills its ISP metric store there, and /metrics/isp is empty. So on those
cameras the day/night value simply never appears - and the thresholds it
exists to help choose, minThreshold and maxThreshold, are left to be guessed
at.

The number does exist, in one of two places depending on what the camera has,
and which one is used matters: whatever is shown here has to be in the same
units as the thresholds the user then types in, or it is worse than showing
nothing at all.

A camera with a photoresistor reads its light from the ADC - which is what
isp.adcReadout says - and that is the number its thresholds are compared
against. One without reads the ISP's own analog gain, published in
/proc/jz/isp as AeAGain, the same quantity majestic's help text names for the
thresholds. Choosing between them by the same setting the switching itself
uses keeps the display and the decision on one scale.

Consulted only when the streamer returned nothing or -1, and the block is
guarded on /proc/jz/isp existing, so no other platform changes behaviour or
pays for the read.

Signed-off-by: Oskari Rauta <oskari.rauta@gmail.com>

--- a/www/cgi-bin/j/pulse.cgi
+++ b/www/cgi-bin/j/pulse.cgi
@@ -6,6 +6,35 @@
 	daynight_value=$(wget -q -T1 localhost/metrics/isp?value=isp_again -O -)
 fi
 
+# On Ingenic the streamer never fills its ISP metric store, so isp_again answers
+# -1 and the day/night reading this page is built around never appears at all.
+# The number does exist, in one of two places, and showing the right one is the
+# whole point: it has to be in the units the thresholds are chosen in.
+#
+#   isp.adcReadout on   the camera has a photoresistor, its light level comes
+#                       from the ADC, and that is what the thresholds compare
+#                       against - so that is what belongs here.
+#   otherwise           the ISP's own analog gain, which is the quantity
+#                       minThreshold and maxThreshold are documented against.
+#
+# Both read higher as it gets darker, so the scale never reverses under the
+# reader. Consulted only when the streamer has nothing to say, and the whole
+# block is guarded on the proc entry existing, so no other platform changes
+# behaviour or pays for the extra read.
+if [ -z "$daynight_value" ] || [ "$daynight_value" = "-1" ]; then
+	if [ -d /proc/jz/isp ]; then
+		if [ "$(cli -g .isp.adcReadout 2>/dev/null)" = "true" ] &&
+		   [ -r /dev/jz_adc_aux_0 ]; then
+			# A binary unsigned long rather than text, so od converts it.
+			# The device name is the one load_ingenic points at whichever
+			# AUX channel this board's sensor is on.
+			daynight_value=$(od -An -tu4 -N4 /dev/jz_adc_aux_0 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' \n')
+		else
+			daynight_value=$(awk '/^AeAGain /{print $3; exit}' /proc/jz/isp/* 2>/dev/null)
+		fi
+	fi
+fi
+
 if [ -n "$temp" ]; then
 	soc_temp="${temp%.*}°C"
 fi
